  <section>
    <h2>Before You Dig: Permits, Property Lines and Utilities</h2>
    <p>Call 811 before any excavation so underground utilities can be marked. Confirm property lines with a survey or existing records. Check with your local building department and homeowners association for height limits, setback requirements, and permit rules. Regulations differ by jurisdiction; do not rely on general guidance alone.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>FAQ</h2>
    <h3>What is a typical fence post spacing?</h3>
    <p>Most wood privacy fences use 8 ft spacing. 6 ft is used for heavier loads or high wind areas. 10 ft is sometimes used with lighter materials or additional bracing.</p>
    <h3>How many posts do I need for 100 ft of fence?</h3>
    <p>At 8 ft spacing, 100 ft requires approximately 14 posts (sections + 1). Exact count depends on gates, corners, and chosen spacing.</p>
    <h3>How much does 100 ft of fence cost?</h3>
    <p>Cost varies widely by material, region, and labor. Material-only estimates range depending on wood, vinyl, or chain link. Always check local supplier pricing.</p>
    <h3>How deep should fence posts be buried?</h3>
    <p>A common rule is to bury one-third of the post length. In frost-prone areas the bottom of the post should extend below the local frost line. Check local building codes for exact requirements.</p>
    <h3>Do I need a permit for a fence?</h3>
    <p>Requirements vary by city and HOA. Many areas require permits for fences above a certain height or near property lines. Contact your local building department and call 811 before digging.</p>
    <h3>Which fence material lasts the longest?</h3>
    <p>Vinyl and aluminum generally require less maintenance and last longer than untreated wood. Treated wood and chain link have intermediate lifespans. Actual longevity depends on climate, installation quality, and maintenance.</p>
  </section>

